Postdoc:  ```PLAN
Title: Adaptive Prompt-Enhanced Score Matching for Partially Observed Data

Objective:
To compare marginal Importance-Weighted (Marg-IW) and marginal Variational (Marg-Var) score matching methods in recovering score functions from partially observed data. We will benchmark parameter estimation and graphical model structure recovery under MCAR (and optionally MNAR) missingness using dynamically adjustable synthetic datasets. A novel meta-learning prompt generator (using a lightweight transformer) will enhance hyperparameter selection and model adaptation on the fly.

Outline:
1. Synthetic Data Generation:
   - Create three families of synthetic models:
     a. Simple multivariate Gaussians with adjustable means and precisions.
     b. An ICA-inspired unnormalizable model defined as qθ(x) ∝ exp(Σθ_i,j x_i² x_j²) with tunable non-linearity.
     c. Gaussian Graphical Models (GGMs) with L1-regularization to induce sparse edge structure.
   - Introduce a “complexity” parameter to each model family that scales intrinsic correlations or non-linearities.
   - Simulate controlled missingness using MCAR, and optionally explore MNAR patterns guided by Appendix A.1.4.

2. Experimental Regimes:
   - Low-Dimensional/Small-n: Favoring Marg-IW due to relatively simple structure.
   - High-Dimensional/Complex: Favoring Marg-Var, capturing richer dependencies.
   - Transitional Regime: A border case to analyze method adaptability.

3. Model Implementations:
   - Develop four variants of score matching in PyTorch:
     • Classical score matching.
     • Truncated score matching (with boundary weights g for stability).
     • Sliced score matching (using random projections).
     • Denoising score matching.
   - Prioritize classical and truncated variants for core experiments and treat sliced and denoising as ablation analyses.
   - Implement an EM-like method (based on Uehara et al., 2020) and a baseline “zeroed” score matching approach for comparative study.

4. Enhanced Prompt Engineering with Meta-Learning:
   - Integrate a lightweight pre-trained transformer as a prompt generator that suggests candidate hyperparameters (e.g., IW sample count r ∈ {5, 10, 50}, variational inner steps L ∈ {1, 5, 10}, learning rates, truncation parameters, and projection counts) and dynamically tunes sliced SM projection parameters.
   - Use meta-learning feedback to adapt the configuration on the fly, reinforcing robust performance across regimes.

5. Experiment Details and Stability:
   - Use reparameterization techniques for variational sampling.
   - Leverage log-sum-exp and gradient clipping to ensure numerical stability in IW computations.
   - Monitor gradient norms and implement weight normalization to stabilize training.

6. Evaluation Metrics:
   - Parameter Estimation: Assess Fisher divergence and parameter estimation error.
   - Graphical Model Recovery: Evaluate edge detection performance using ROC curves and AUC in the GGMs.
   - Report training stability and hyperparameter sensitivity, including analyses from the meta-learning prompt generator.

7. Reproducibility and Reporting:
   - Ensure reproducibility by fixing random seeds, logging detailed software/hardware configurations, and pinning package versions.
   - Provide comprehensive ablation studies comparing Marg-IW, Marg-Var, the EM-like method, and the baseline across all regimes.

Deliverables:
   - A clean experimental framework in PyTorch.
   - Synthetic datasets with adjustable complexity for all three model families.
   - Implementation of the four score matching variants, meta-learning prompt mechanism, and baseline methods.
   - Detailed analytical report with performance metrics, ablation analyses, and discussions on hyperparameter robustness.

This plan leverages innovative meta-learning for prompt engineering alongside rigorous experimental benchmarking to produce cutting-edge insights into score matching with partially observed data.
  
Good luck with the implementation and evaluation!
  
Feel free to discuss any additional refinements.
  
Submitted.
  
PLAN
``` 
